<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-normal-line-eqn-R2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Normal vector to a line in <m> \R^2 </m> from its equation</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	a linear equation corresponding to a line in <m> \R^2 </m> and two points <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> are provided.
	<ol>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-orthog-normal-line-eqn-R2-normal-vec">
		By inspection,
		determine a <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-normal-vector" text="custom">normal vector</xref></term> for the line from its equation.
		</li>

		<li> Verify that points <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> lie the line. </li>

		<li>
		Compute the vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> associated to directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m>,
		and then verify that <m> \uvec{v} </m> is orthogonal to your normal vector from <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-orthog-normal-line-eqn-R2-normal-vec" text="type-local">part</xref>.
		</li>

		<li>
		Sketch the line in the <m> xy </m>-plane,
		along with points <m> P, Q </m> and vector <m> \uvec{v} </m>.
		Then draw your normal vector with its initial point at one of either <m> P </m> or <m> Q </m>.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> \ell \colon \:\: 3 x - 5 y = -2 </m></p>

		<p><m> P(-4,-2), Q(1,1) </m></p>

	</statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p> The line equation coefficients form a normal vector: <md> \uvec{n} = (3,-5) </md>. </p></li>

		<li><p><md>

			<mrow> P \amp \colon \:\: 3 \cdot (-4) - 5 \cdot (-2) = -2 </mrow>

			<mrow> Q \amp \colon \:\: 3 \cdot 1 - 5 \cdot 1 = -2 </mrow>

		</md></p></li>

		<li><p>
			<m> \uvec{v} = (5,3) </m>
			<md> \udotprod{n}{v} = 3 \cdot 5 + (-5) \cdot 3 = 0 </md>,
			hence orthogonal.
		</p></li>

		<li><image width="76.4%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-normal-line-eqn-R2-3-neg5-neg2">
				<xi:include href="normal-line-eqn-R2.d/3-neg5-neg2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a normal vector to a line in the plane. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a line with positive slope,
				a vector parallel to it,
				and a vector normal to it.
				The graph of the line <m> 3 x - 5 y = -2 </m> is sketched and labelled as <m> \ell </m>.
				passing through plotted points <m> P(-4,-2) </m> and <m> Q(1,1) </m>.
				The directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m> is drawn and labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m>.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{n} </m> extends from <m> Q </m> downwards and rightwards,
				forming a right angle with line <m> \ell </m> at <m> Q </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> \ell \colon \:\: 2 x + 4 y = -5 </m>,</p>

		<p><m> P(0, -5/4), Q(-5, 5/4) </m></p>

	</statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p> The line equation coefficients form a normal vector: <md> \uvec{n} = (2,4) </md>. </p></li>

		<li><p><md>

			<mrow> P \amp \colon \:\: 2 \cdot 0 + 4 \cdot (- 5 / 4) = -5 </mrow>

			<mrow> Q \amp \colon \:\: 2 \cdot (-5) + 4 \cdot (5 / 4) = -5 </mrow>

		</md></p></li>

		<li><p>
			<m> \uvec{v} = (-5, 5/2) </m>
			<md> \udotprod{n}{v} = 2 \cdot (-5) + 4 \cdot (5 / 2) = 0 </md>,
			hence orthogonal.
		</p></li>

		<li><image width="72.3%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-normal-line-eqn-R2-2-4-neg5">
				<xi:include href="normal-line-eqn-R2.d/2-4-neg5.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a normal vector to a line in the plane. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a line with negative slope,
				a vector parallel to it,
				and a vector normal to it.
				The graph of the line <m> 2 x + 4 y = -5 </m> is sketched and labelled as <m> \ell </m>.
				passing through plotted points <m> P(0,-5/4) </m> and <m> Q(-5,5/4) </m>.
				The directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m> is drawn and labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m>.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{n} </m> extends from <m> P </m> upwards and rightwards,
				forming a right angle with line <m> \ell </m> at <m> P </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
